The **ChildNode.wrapWith()** method would replace the child node with an element and append the child node to the element.

### Syntax

```
ChildNode.wrapWith(Element);
```

### Examples

Wrapping a `<button>` in a `<form>`:

```html
<button>View More</button>
```

```js
const buttonEl = document.querySelector('button');

const formEl = document.createElement('form');
const hiddenEl = document.createElement('input');

hiddenEl.name = 'some-arg';
hiddenEl.type = 'hidden';
hiddenEl.value = 'some-value';

formEl.append(hiddenEl);

buttonEl.wrapWith(formEl);
```

```html
<form>
  <input name="some-arg" type="hidden" value="some-value">
  <button>View More</button>
</form>
```

[Re-worked MDN `replaceWith` example](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/ChildNode/replaceWith#Using_replaceWith()):

```js
var parent = document.createElement("div");
var child = document.createElement("p");
var span = document.createElement("span");

span.wrapWith(child);
child.wrapWith(parent);

console.log(parent.outerHTML);
// "<div><p><span></span></p></div>"
```

### Rationale:

Prior art for this exists in Bliss.js as [`around`](http://blissfuljs.com/#quick-look) and in jQuery as [`wrap`](http://api.jquery.com/wrap/).

Like `childNode.remove()`, we’re de-complicating the current process of wrapping a node, e.g.:

```js
if (childNode.parentNode) {
  childNode.parentNode.insertBefore(element, childNode).appendChild(childNode);
}
```
